Notebook[{Cell["Reading diagrams from the book", "Title"], 
  Cell["How a figure of Turaev & Virelizier becomes data that the CCN String \
Diagrams code can draw, type and simplify", "Subtitle"], 
  Cell["By Dara O Shayda and Noor Hoshino (\:661f\:91ce\:30cc\:30fc\:30eb) \
\[CenterDot] \[Copyright] Computational Classnotes \[CenterDot] CC BY-NC-SA \
4.0", "Text"], Cell["The pictures in this notebook are crops of the book's \
pages, from Turaev & Virelizier, Monoidal Categories and Topological Field \
Theory (Progress in Mathematics 322, Springer 2017), \[Copyright] Springer \
International Publishing AG 2017. They are short excerpts, shown for study and commentary beside the book.", "Text"], Cell["1. The method", 
   "Section"], Cell["No program reads the figures. A person (or any software or online service) looks \
at each figure, writes it down as data, and then lets the code check the \
transcription. Five steps:", "Text"], Cell["1. Get the page as an image. \
Render the PDF page (pdftoppm, or Import with Rasterize) and crop the figure. \
The text around it comes from the PDF's text layer.", "Item"], 
  Cell["2. Read it from the bottom up, as the book draws it. The strands \
crossing the bottom edge are the source, read left to right. Then go up box \
by box: its name, which strands go in (counted from the left), which come \
out. Caps are ev, \:1ebdv; cups are coev, c\:0303oev; crossings are \[Tau], \
\[Tau]\:207b\271. In the pivotal calculus (\[Section]2.1.3) an arc pointing \
down is coloured X, an arc pointing up contributes X*.", "Item"], 
  Cell["3. Use the text. It says what each box is (f : X \[RightArrow] Y, a \
pairing, a partition) and often gives the formula the figure stands for. That \
settles what the picture leaves open, such as the order of boxes at the same \
height.", "Item"], Cell["4. Write it down: as editor text (\"(id_X \
\[CircleTimes] ev_X)(coev_X \[CircleTimes] id_X)\") or directly as a layered \
form LD[source, {{a, B[name, ins, outs]}, \[Ellipsis]}] listing the boxes \
bottom to top, a being the number of strands to the left of the box.", 
   "Item"], Cell["5. Check it. (a) It fits together: every box's inputs are \
the strands below it (validQ, and the type checker of expressionToLD). (b) It \
looks the same: draw it (render, renderP) beside the book's figure. (c) It \
computes the same: the rules take one side of the book's equation to the \
other (equationReport), and for the harder results the matrix models of the \
notebooks agree.", "Item"], Cell["Most reading errors are in step 2: a \
crossing read the wrong way, an arrow missed, a box put on the wrong strand. \
Step 5 is there to catch them. Section 10 shows two such errors being \
caught.", "Text"], Cell["2. Setup", "Section"], 
  Cell["Evaluate once. simplifier.wl loads expression-to-diagram.wl and \
diagram-to-expression.wl, and the rewriting engine. The helper bookAndOurs \
puts the book's picture beside our drawing.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \
\"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"simplifier.wl\"}]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["vee[text_, sig_] := expressionToLD[parseExpression[text], \
parseSignature[sig], \"Duals\" -> \"vee\"];\nbookAndOurs[img_, lds_List, r_ : \
renderP] := Grid[{{img, Row[Riffle[Show[r[fromLayered[#]], ImageSize -> \
{UpTo[150], UpTo[120]}] & /@ lds, Style[\"  =  \", 16]]]}, {\"the book\", \
\"our transcription\"}}, Spacings -> {3, 1}, Alignment -> \
Center];\nbookImage[name_String] := \
ToExpression[First[NotebookRead[First[Cells[EvaluationNotebook[], CellTags -> \
name]]]]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["3. Figure 2.1: level exchange (\[Section]2.1.1, p. 32)", "Section"], 
